    "claim": "Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is more likely true, but as a critical rationalist rule of action: we act on the theory that has withstood the most severe tests because it offers the best available (though fallible) grasp of reality, while explicitly rejecting the notion that this survival increases its logical probability. The cost of accepting this view is the abandonment of 'probabilistic security'—the comfort of believing our actions are backed by high statistical likelihood—and the acceptance that all scientific action remains a bold conjecture, even when based on extensive corroboration.",
